When `next()` runs and activeCount hits 0 with empty queue, a job's result promise has just settled. Any continuation that enqueues more work from that settlement will run in the same microtask drain. If we resolve `onIdle` synchronously, then `await limit.onIdle()` continuation is also queued as a microtask — it would run after already-queued continuations but potentially interleave. Hmm, actually if we resolve synchronously, the onIdle waiters' continuations get queued after all currently-queued microtasks. The user's enqueue continuation might already be queued before. So a synchronous resolve might actually be fine for the adoption case.

